#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534983 :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534999 :question/problem "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(x+2) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+3 & =\\frac{5}{6}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{6}{5}(y+3) & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{36}{25}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{36}{25}{(y+3)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+2)}^{2}+{(y+3)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{36}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535000 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a5f23af5dd514a65937eee29bb0992ff94d68897d8c6422fcf215aad45379c4a.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works with the particle for $y<0,$ while $F$  works against the particle for $y>0.$ \n\n![](images/53/537aca6190fc2f3e06e85c0781a8bb461559ef0a7515ee29aca505f9d615f820.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $y<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $y>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/49/4939f9a22400a7a56eaeacb77d520e522f50aa8378ca1014a19810122e12da51.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535004 :question/problem "![](images/33/33070b4b871552c4c45f21f0885cbce45ff65eb278cb000e68c91adfc2ab004c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $16$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(16,0)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=16.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535012 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535014 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =16(1) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535015 :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcf7a2ea5b44f3f5ba7b1663f7979cdc3fc8c1036b6cc797904ffda77f1003bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/81/818ad935fd7aa592f00c23c9c77799d127449b4bb6ffacee4354e7acf39887d8.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/70/70e268a08195a92573f41927ae7de653837fca273f0c75cb951243a31ec660a2.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535016 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca3a73d411d7b766196a2bebf56f9d678aba54a4f2265ac08c36d0fb3fbf03b0.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/37/37665aa7ea5388a1162ca68a1662aaa86d6369f083d7563dbf27c521c13a7c72.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535031 :question/problem "![](images/79/795d51d5695c48f5d12c9057030f6a48e58a579fb262a83afc132425ec7668b3.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/a7/a78203eda3869c4478a97da0875dda7e72717911a6e1aee524bb963533a5e12e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535047 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z={e}^{x}$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $x\\le y\\le x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}y]}_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2x}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{3}(1+{e}^{2x})^{3/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((1+{e}^{2})^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535052 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $\\frac{1}{3}$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535056 :question/problem "![](images/15/158ce9317d062a090ed36bda7361d025e4c8f4c68ea43484f50015f01c411a30.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/5c/5cc99d78c5fe5afacde4ba96e2a4010b0fab66bdabbe5c2b518bd936f36c7d84.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535070 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(-5,-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535075 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-3x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $3x\\le y\\le 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-3x)=-3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-3x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{z}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{1+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{3x}^{3}\\sqrt{y-3x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{2}{3}(y-3x)^{3/2}]}_{y=3x}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-3x)^{3/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{3}\\,{[\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)(3-3x)^{5/2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(0-{3}^{5/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{11}}{45}(9\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{33}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535078 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/d8/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/17/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535079 :question/problem "![](images/3c/3c2035d50b823b9bcbeb3ae8cf698df9a25c95a289c5a6f35054269dc920088d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/df/dfec76b47e8508a57e18440a72986bcae8e2bd19559bd0915ea4530e0b08b4f6.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/58/58e3efc39d0aee02d14e7c80c7355c37a1f28b6f0f54ff7102553e3a6ffc17cf.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535084 :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2},$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{2}y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂x}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{∂z}{∂y}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(1)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2(x+\\sqrt{2}y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,2({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}xy)\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535086 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,3)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $3$ centered at the point $(-2,3).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535156 :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c)$, if $4a\\cdot (b\\times c)=12.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =12 \\\\ 4\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =12 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535166 :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (c\\times a)$, if $2a\\cdot (b\\times c)=-12.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-12 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-12 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535167 :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot ((-4)c\\times 3a)$, if $4a\\cdot (2b\\times (-4)c)=-128.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a\\cdot (2b\\times (-4)c) & =-128 \\\\ 4\\cdot 2\\cdot (-4)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-128 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring out the constants in the requested product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot ((-4)c\\times 3a) & =(-4)\\cdot 3[b\\cdot (c\\times a)] \\\\ & =(-4)\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535186 :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c)$, if $(-7)a\\cdot (b\\times c)=-28.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7)a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-28 \\\\ (-7)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-28 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535190 :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (a\\times b)$, if $(-2)a\\cdot (b\\times c)=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2)a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =6 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =6 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $b$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535192 :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot ((-2)a\\times 4b)$, if $(-5)a\\cdot (4b\\times (-4)c)=320.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-5)a\\cdot (4b\\times (-4)c) & =320 \\\\ (-5)\\cdot 4\\cdot (-4)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =320 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $b$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring out the constants in the requested product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot ((-2)a\\times 4b) & =(-2)\\cdot 4[c\\cdot (a\\times b)] \\\\ & =(-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\ & =-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535195 :question/problem "Find $a\\cdot (3c\\times 4b)$, if $2a\\cdot (2b\\times 3c)=108.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (2b\\times 3c) & =108 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =108 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $b$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (c\\times b) & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring out the constants in the requested product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (3c\\times 4b) & =3\\cdot 4[a\\cdot (c\\times b)] \\\\ & =3\\cdot 4\\cdot (-9) \\\\ & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535198 :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (a\\times c)$, if $2a\\cdot (b\\times c)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =2 \\\\ 2\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =2 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535203 :question/problem "Find $b\\cdot (2c\\times 4a)$, if $3a\\cdot ((-5)b\\times (-3)c)=135.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a\\cdot ((-5)b\\times (-3)c) & =135 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-5)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =135 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $a$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (c\\times a) & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring out the constants in the requested product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (2c\\times 4a) & =2\\cdot 4[b\\cdot (c\\times a)] \\\\ & =2\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\ & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535204 :question/problem "Find $c\\cdot (2a\\times (-4)b)$, if $4a\\cdot ((-4)b\\times (-3)c)=-240.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a\\cdot ((-4)b\\times (-3)c) & =-240 \\\\ 4\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-3)\\cdot [a\\cdot (b\\times c)] & =-240 \\\\ a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $a$ and $b$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot (a\\times c) & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $b$ and $c$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (a\\times b) & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring out the constants in the requested product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot (2a\\times (-4)b) & =2\\cdot (-4)[c\\cdot (a\\times b)] \\\\ & =2\\cdot (-4)\\cdot (-5) \\\\ & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535253 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-1)+1,\\,y=t(2-t)+1,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-1)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(2-t)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/4b/4b2ef1b94068ecd2db753e66684d7f7a38239a2a525fe37456b58ee87531a616.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535255 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-1)-2,\\,y=t(2-t)+2,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-1)-2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(2-t)+2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $4$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $-6$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/55/555c7b92a2cd68425b5b096ac55597d0e78e05364bb8706c1b15da88af99bdcb.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535257 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-1)+1,\\,y=t(2-t)-2,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-1)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(2-t)-2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the 