\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-2)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(1-t)+2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $9$ | $4$ | $1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/1b/1beefdb775fb75458624bc095771e454faf5101270a4c4d9834bf5eef2238910.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535273 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-2)-1,\\,y=t(1-t)-2,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-2)-1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(1-t)-2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $7$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-4$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/67/671f539b58c5eeed927f86b97a2893f44a7bc9e6c0f776315e3f8c92090c4a0e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535279 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-3)+1,\\,y=t(2-t)+1,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-3)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $11$ | $5$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(2-t)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $11$ | $5$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/6d/6d3d8c8d18a1b5b31d33376e45323817676f282272f9d4965fb856ca1954f349.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535287 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-1)+2,\\,y=t(2-t)+1,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-1)+2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(2-t)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $8$ | $4$ | $2$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/3f/3f8daefef362bb0fbb96618f31e9f5ad912adedbc0eda5592b2ab1fafbc71577.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535294 :question/problem "Determine the graph of the following parametrically defined curve:\n\n$$\nx=t(t-3)+2,\\,y=t(1-t)+1,\\,-2\\le t\\le 2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-3)+2.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $12$ | $6$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t(1-t)+1.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $12$ | $6$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](images/f9/f94e0cba520e106ebd4096364812bc11a41383868d7d6e07537dc317b0f4c939.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535305 :question/problem "![](images/38/38947ad6e5f10e1e25d3d10777ad28c47aac1f0c0f311621c0194bef815331ab.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ \\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta 2 (r\\cos\\theta) (r\\sin\\theta) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535318 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a332a1bea58e47ab58fa47c6944213ce4caa5d26e6bfb129fea613adcd524b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta 2 (r\\cos\\theta) (r\\sin\\theta) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
